    <section>
        <h3>Answer 1</h3>
        <div class="question-box">What is polymorphism in OOP?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p>Treating different object types through a shared interface/superclass so the same code works for multiple types.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Answer 2</h3>
        <div class="question-box">What is the difference between composition and aggregation?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p>Composition is a strong has‑a relationship (part depends on whole). Aggregation is weaker (part can exist independently).</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Answer 3</h3>
        <div class="question-box">What is the purpose of an interface?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p>To enforce a contract: implementing classes must provide specific method signatures.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 3. THE HOOK (R-STACK) -->
<section>
    <h2>The Big Question</h2>
    <div class="r-stack">
        <div class="fragment fade-in-then-out">
            <div class="question-box">
                <p style="font-size:1.25em;">🤔 Why do software teams keep solving the same design problems?</p>
                <p style="font-size:1.1em; margin-top:0.8em;">Creating objects, managing shared resources, reacting to events…</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment fade-in-then-out">
            <div class="box" style="background:#ffebee;">
                <p style="font-size:1.15em;"><strong>❌ Misconception:</strong> “A design pattern is copy‑paste code.”</p>
                <p class="small-text">Patterns are reusable ideas and structures, not a single fixed implementation.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment fade-in">
            <div class="box-blue">
                <p style="font-size:1.15em;"><strong>✅ Key idea:</strong> a <span class="keyword" data-def="Design pattern: reusable general solution/structure to a commonly occurring design problem.">design pattern</span> is a proven solution template.</p>
                <p class="small-text">It improves clarity, consistency, and maintainability across a codebase.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 4. SINGLETON -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Singleton pattern: restricts a class to a single instance and provides global access to it.">Singleton</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">What if your program must have exactly one instance of something?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Goal:</strong> ensure only one instance exists.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Use:</strong> configuration manager, logging service, DB connection manager.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Trade-offs</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Can become “global state” (harder to test).</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Thread safety can be tricky in concurrent programs.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">You want one shared logger across the entire application. Which pattern fits best?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Singleton</strong></p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 5. FACTORY -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Factory pattern: creates objects through a common interface without specifying the exact class in client code.">Factory</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">How can we create objects without hard-coding concrete classes everywhere?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Goal:</strong> centralise object creation.</p>
            <p class="fragment">Client code depends on an interface (supports polymorphism).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-gears"></i> Example idea</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Create <code>Circle</code>, <code>Square</code>, <code>Triangle</code> based on a string input.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">You need to create different “PaymentMethod” objects based on user choice. Which pattern fits best?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Factory</strong></p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 6. OBSERVER -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Observer pattern: a subscription mechanism where observers are notified automatically when a subject’s state changes.">Observer</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">How can many parts of a program react to one event without tight coupling?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Subject</strong> maintains a list of observers.</p>
            <p class="fragment">When state changes, the subject notifies observers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-bell"></i> Where you see it</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">UI events (button click → many handlers)</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Realtime dashboards (data update → multiple views refresh)</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">Multiple widgets must update when a value changes. Which pattern fits best?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Observer</strong></p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 7. MISCONCEPTION CHECK -->
<section>
    <section>
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-check-double"></i> Misconception Check</h2>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A.</strong> Design patterns are mandatory rules for all programs.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B.</strong> Singleton enforces a single instance of a class.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C.</strong> Factory can reduce coupling by hiding concrete class construction.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D.</strong> Observer supports event-driven updates to multiple subscribers.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-text" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.6em;">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-down blue"></i> <span class="blue">Press Down</span> for answers
        </p>
    </section>

    <section data-background="#f4f6f8">
        <h3>Answers</h3>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A: False.</strong> They are optional tools; use them when they fit the problem.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B: True.</strong> That’s its definition.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C: True.</strong> Creation is centralised behind an interface.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D: True.</strong> Observers are notified when the subject changes.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 8. SUMMARY -->
<section data-background="linear-gradient(135deg, #0E214B 0%, #1a3a7a 100%)">
    <h2 style="color: white;">Key Takeaways</h2>
    <div class="box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: white;">
        <ul style="line-height:1.5;">
            <li><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> Design patterns are reusable solution templates for recurring problems.</li>
            <li><strong>Singleton</strong>: one instance, shared access.</li>
            <li><strong>Factory</strong>: flexible object creation behind an interface.</li>
            <li><strong>Observer</strong>: event-driven notifications to multiple subscribers.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
